[{:seon.task/name "a-long-string-does-not-starve-its-siblings"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: an eval item {:form \"(code-of 0)\" :out <9,600 chars of pprint output> :value (vec (range 2000))} printed under 545 tokens (`seon.print/print-value`, the last-evals share) shows 1,584 characters of :out and :value as `[…2000 (get-in (nth (my.turn/evals) 0) [:value])]`: not one element of the value the agent asked for. Cause: `seon.print.select` ranks a long string before a collection (`member-rank`) and then allots the string's characters like one member, greedily, while the collection's members are a level deeper, so breadth-first never reaches them. Correct: a long string's characters are allotted in the same rounds as its siblings' members (a string is a sequence of lines for allotment), so every sibling shows its first part before any one deepens. Every value with a long string beside a collection benefits (contract errors, test expected/actual, the context). Replaces nothing; the law tests in seon.print-test stay green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-test/a-long-string-shares-the-budget-with-its-siblings}}
 {:seon.task/name "numbers-are-formatted-by-the-printer"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the one-offs survey (the archived paper one-offs-2026-10-23.md (git history); owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs, DECISIONS). Item 8. What exists: seon.cost/num-text and seon.render.ui's fmt1, fmt2 and group3 format numbers beside the printer, and seon.repl rounds to three places inline beside seon.lib/r2. Correct: one number formatter, the printer's, which the renders and the cost verdict call."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "breadth-shows-no-member-that-shows-nothing"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the printer audit (docs/render.md; owner, 2026-10-25: elision placeholders should not be printed). ```seon.print.select/fill-rows``` shows the members of one class whole then one cut, but a collection of such members with one of another kind falls back to ```seon.print.select/fill-members```, which places every member at its least first: 177 maps and a keyword print 177 lines of `{…7 (get-in result/e1 [N])}`. Correct: breadth places a member whose least form shows nothing only where a later member that shows something needs its position; a run of them at the tail is the collection's one cut. Proof: (assoc maps 3 :odd) of 177 maps prints no `{…` line and one cut past the members shown."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-test/breadth-shows-no-member-that-shows-nothing}}
 {:seon.task/name "printing-costs-few-contracted-calls"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-30 (jolt/agent-costs, 6adfd086bf: bin/test now arms what root arms at boot, so cost proofs count as an agent's child does): printing through the armed printer is most of some functions' contracted calls. seon.context/printed-run went from 16 counted calls to 560, seon.context/request from 9 to 327, seon.render.ui/html from 19 to 144 per run, every one validated by its contract in root and agents. The printer's per-element helpers (seon.print, seon.print.emit, seon.print.select) are contracted and called per value printed, so a page or a request pays a contract check per element. Plan: measure which printer vars take the calls (seon.profile/touched over printed-run), then make the per-element step a local function of one contracted entry (as seon.merge/content-attr builds its predicate once a merge read, batch 20), never an uncontracted var. Proof: printed-run's calls fall back near 16, its estimate with it."
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-one-printer-is-for-context"
  :seon.task/plan "What is wrong: code prints values where it should keep them as data. The owner, 2026-11-04: \"Printed data is rarely the right choice and I want us to be very suspricious of any code that is printing values. The only value printer should be the one value printer and that's for assembling context for agents\". Sweep of src, 2026-11-04, on the step 6 branch, grep of pr-str, print-str, with-out-str and seon.print's entries: 71 lines of code. Context or page through the one printer, fine, 28: seon.context/eval-messages, message-messages, page-message; seon.repl's response text; seon.server's tool text; seon.render's print-html and nested; seon.render.ui's lists; seon.render.proc's root context; seon.web.html's fallback; my.agent's sizer. A value printed into what is kept, where data could be, should go, 15: seon.repl/record-details's :printed and :line; seon.repl's :seon.error/given; seon.agent's test failures (:expected, :actual, :got, :message); my.task/fail!'s :counterexample; seon.turn's message bodies (two); seon.contract/plainly (two: it prints what it cannot keep) and two of seon.contract's error values; seon.render.proc's detail. Words for a reader, an error's message or a form to run, each to read again, 24: seon.repl's edit! messages (five), seon.contract's explain text (four), seon.cost's verdict lines (three), seon.server's (four), seon.agent's (two), seon.context's search hint, seon.task's refused entry, my.schema's, my.agent's, seon.main's, seon.repl's stderr. A wire or a key, not a value printed, 4: seon.edn/encode, the one encoder; a schema form's text in seon.context's search; my.root's form sent to an agent; root's warm-up. What should be true: what is kept is data, up to a maximum the config states (the core's spec, with :printed and :line going), and what prints a value does it through the one printer, for a reader, when read. Change none of this before the core: its spec holds the first group of changes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-test/the-one-printer-is-for-context}}]
